+Bounding the router's memory
+----------------------------
+
+The router finds prefix matches in a trie of hashed 128-character chunks that it builds from its own dispatch history. By default the trie forgets nothing, so on a workload with ever-new prompts (long-document QA, for instance) it grows for as long as the router runs, and a router pod with a memory limit is eventually restarted (`#687 <https://github.com/vllm-project/production-stack/issues/687>`_).
+
+``--prefix-max-chunks-per-endpoint`` (``routerSpec.prefixMaxChunksPerEndpoint`` in the Helm chart) bounds the trie: once an endpoint is listed on more chunks than this, its least recently used chunks are forgotten, the tail chunks of a prompt before its head chunks. That is also the order in which a vLLM instance frees a finished request's KV blocks, so the trie's picture of each endpoint's cache ages roughly the way the cache itself does, and a chunk the router has forgotten is one the engine would have evicted around the same time.
+
+Size the bound from the engine's own KV cache, which ``/metrics`` reports in ``vllm:cache_config_info`` as ``num_gpu_blocks`` and ``block_size``: the cache holds ``num_gpu_blocks * block_size`` tokens, and one chunk of 128 characters is about 32 tokens of English text. For an instance with 100,000 blocks of 16 tokens, 1,600,000 tokens, that is 50,000 chunks:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   vllm-router ... --routing-logic prefixaware --prefix-max-chunks-per-endpoint 50000
+
+The router then holds at most 50,000 chunk nodes per endpoint. A bound smaller than the engine's cache makes the router forget prefixes the engine still holds; a bound larger than it keeps the router routing to prefixes the engine has already evicted, which is what an unbounded trie does all the time.
+
